  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 942 PM MDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nWIDESPREAD PRECIPITATION MONDAY + TUESDAY:\n\nMoisture advection from the tropical storm activity in the Eastern \nPacific will bring anomalous moisture to our region through at least \nmidweek. PWAT anomalies will be upwards of 250% of normal for a good \nchunk of the CWA, especially south of I-70. This means a return to \nwidespread precipitation chances for much of the region. Shower \nactivity is expected to start in the Four Corners area in the early \nmorning hours on Monday, spreading northward by sunrise. \nAdditionally, this moisture will be accompanied by synoptic forcing, \nas a closed upper level low digs southward through California on \nMonday, and then across the Desert Southwest on Tuesday and \nWednesday. A jet streak aloft and energy from a trough axis will \nwork in tandem to create lift for showers and storms. This means \nthere may be enough forcing from those factors to create embedded \nthunderstorms, but even recent HiRes guidance continues to favor the \nstratiform rainfall storm mode. The potential for widespread \nprecipitation sticks around for Tuesday as well before the trough \naxis begins tracking eastwards across the Divide by Wednesday.\n\nThe potential for prolonged periods of rainfall, including in areas \nthat have recently received substantial rainfall poses flooding \nconcerns for the coming days. Hence, a Flood Watch is in effect for \nsoutheast Utah and southwest Colorado from 6AM Monday through 6AM \nWednesday. Please see the hydrology section below for more \ninformation on those flooding concerns. Additionally, if you \nplan on recreating outdoors over the next few days, continue to \nkeep on eye on the forecast and make yourself aware of any \nactive watches or warnings in your area. \n\nRainfall amounts carry some uncertainty because models disagree on \nthe exact track of the closed low and key areas of forcing. Still, \nsouth- and southwest-facing slopes in the San Juans are favored. \nMost of the Four Corners area has an 80-90% chance of exceeding 1\" \nof rain by 6AM Wednesday, while locations like Durango, Pagosa \nSprings, and Ignacio have a 60-70% chance of exceeding 2.5\" for the \nsame time period. \n\nLastly, increased cloud cover will knock temperatures down quite a \nbit from Sunday's highs. Most spots will stay cooler than average \nfor the first half of the work week.   \n\nMOISTURE BEGINS TO EXIT THE REGION WEDNESDAY:\n\nBy Wednesday, the elevated moisture and closed low will move east of \nthe Rocky Mountains, bringing back drier weather for the area. \nHowever, lingering moisture may be enough to support additional \nconvection over the higher terrain Wednesday afternoon.",
